# rhyremix "no pops" A/B profile - n01. Install with:
#   tools/rtxprofile.sh nopops [GAME_DIR]        undo: tools/rtxprofile.sh restore [GAME_DIR]
#
# This is config/rtx.conf with ONLY the temporal keys changed: the keys that decide WHEN a
# lighting change lands, not what the light looks like. It exists to be A/B'd against
# `current` with scripts/lighting-test.sh (the drive-through of the 2026-11-12 test session),
# because the tour takes every shot at a standstill and therefore cannot see a transition.
#
# Evidence for everything below: .strata/notes-n01-lighting-no-pops-renderer.md step 2, which
# lists the keys of config/rtx.conf with their values in <game>/rtx.conf.orig (the runtime's own
# first-launch file), the verdict of tools/rtxkeycheck.py against config/rtx-runtime-keys.txt,
# and the tooltip each key carries in the runtime's strings.
#
# Which temporal path is live here, from <game>/rtx-remix/logs/remix-dxvk.log:
#   "Integrate Indirect Mode: Spatially Hashed Radiance Cache - activated"  (SHaRC)
#   "NVIDIA DLSS-NR support query failed (0xbad00010)"                      (no Ray Reconstruction)
#   "[RTX Neural Radiance Cache] Selected Ultra preset mode."               (trained, not queried)
# So the live time path is SHaRC + the RTXDI (rtx.di.*) direct-light resampler + the volumetric
# accumulator + the final temporal AA. The rtx.restirGI.* block the shipped file carries belongs
# to an integrator this install does not run.
#
# CHANGED - nine keys, all of them temporal:
#   rtx.di.enableTemporalReuse = True              "Whether to apply temporal reuse."
#   rtx.di.enableTemporalBiasCorrection = True     "Corrects bias caused by temporal reprojection."
#       Direct light is what this plugin publishes (one distant light per body, the lamps, the
#       signal heads). Reusing samples across frames is what makes a change converge over frames
#       instead of arriving at full strength in one.
#   rtx.spatiallyHashedRadianceCache.maxNumFramesToAccumulate = 16
#       "Maximum number of frames accumulated in each SHaRC entry. Higher values reduce noise but
#        respond more slowly to lighting changes."
#   rtx.volumetrics.maxAccumulationFrames = 16
#       "The number of frames to accumulate volume lighting samples over, maximum of 254. Large
#        values result in greater image stability at the cost of potentially more temporal lag."
#       (rtx.enableVolumetricLighting = True is in this file, so light shafts are on the table.)
#   rtx.temporalAA.newFrameWeight = 0.35
#       "The maximum amount of the current frame to use as part of the temporal anti-aliasing
#        process ... closer to 0 will result in better image stability ... closer to 1 will
#        result in more responsive results (less ghosting)." The one whole-image lever here.
#   rtx.autoExposure.autoExposureSpeed = 0.5
#       "Average exposure changing speed (in units per second) when the image changes." Inert
#       while rtx.autoExposure.enabled stays False; set so that if exposure is ever switched on
#       it ramps instead of snapping.
#   rtx.restirGI.historyDiscardStrength             10 -> 2
#   rtx.restirGI.useAdaptiveTemporalHistory     False -> True
#   rtx.restirGI.validateVisibilityChange         True -> False
#       "The sensitivity of discarding history. Higher values discard more history." / "Adjust
#        temporal history length based on frame rate." / "Remove samples when visibility has
#        changed." These are the amplifiers s28 named plus their sibling: at 10 with adaptive
#        history off, a lighting change throws the temporal history away and lands at full
#        strength the next frame. They are INERT while SHaRC is the integration mode, and are
#        aligned anyway so the A/B covers them and the file is right if the integrator changes.
#
# DELIBERATELY NOT CHANGED:
#   rtx.demodulate.enableDirectLightBoilingFilter stays False. Its tooltip is "Boiling filter
#       removing direct light sample when its luminance is too high" - every light this plugin
#       publishes is direct light, and tour shots 02-04 of 2026-11-12 are sodium lamps at night:
#       the filter would remove the brightest samples of exactly the shots rhY called good.
#   rtx.di.permutationSamplingNthFrame is carried through unchanged. n01 left it at
#       1 ("a noise knob, not a lighting-step knob"); n04 moved the SHIPPED value to 0,
#       because "Apply permutation sampling when (frameIdx % this == 0), 0 means off"
#       re-randomises which lights a pixel draws every frame and over the 400-550 lamp
#       pool this plugin keeps live that is finding A. nopops inherits whatever the
#       shipped file says, so the A/B stays a temporal-only diff.
#   rtx.usePostFilter / rtx.postFilterThreshold stay False / 10 (an NRC training-clamp pair).
#   Every look key is byte-identical to config/rtx.conf, including the n16 tonemap block
#       (tonemappingMode 0 = Global, tonemap.exposureBias -0.5, localtonemap.shadows 1.0,
#       localtonemap.highlights 2.0, tonemap.finalizeWithACES True). n16 changed the shipped
#       localtonemap.shadows from 2.5 to 1.0 - it amplifies a step in direct light, and at 2.5 it
#       lifted the floor - and this profile inherits whatever the shipped file says, so the A/B
#       stays a temporal-only diff either way. Also inherited unchanged: skyBrightness,
#       emissiveIntensity, translucentMaterial.normalIntensity, sceneScale, zUp,
#       ignoreGameDirectionalLights, graphicsPreset, and all eight texture-hash lists.
#       scripts/rtxprofile-selftest.sh hashes the lists and asserts the look keys and
#       graphicsPreset come through unchanged.
#   The n24 night keys are inherited the same way (fallbackLightMode 0 + fallbackLightRadiance
#       0,0,0 with the old type/radius commented out, enableTransmissionApproximationInIndirectRays
#       False, tonemap.shadowContrast 0.4, bloom.luminanceThreshold 1.0). None of them is
#       temporal: they decide what the night is lit BY, not when a change lands, so this A/B
#       must not move them - and a nopops run that shows a different night than `current` is a
#       bug in this file, not a finding.
#   WHY n16 MATTERS FOR THIS A/B: with rtx.tonemappingMode = 0 the local tonemapper is not
#       dispatched at all (rtx_local_tone_mapping.cpp:144), so the whole-image curve in
#       rtx_tone_mapping.cpp is what runs, and that curve is mixed with the previous frame's at
#       exp(-0.07) - about 14 frames to settle (tonemapping_tone_curve.comp.slang, "Temporal
#       filtering"). That is a second, independent smoothing of a lighting change, on top of the
#       nine temporal keys this profile moves. Read the A/B with that in mind: if a step now lags,
#       it may be the curve, not the reservoirs.
#   The n17 atmosphere block is carried through byte-identical (enableFogRemap True, the four
#       fogRemap bounds 400/4000 m in and 2/6 m out, fogRemapColorMultiscatteringScale 0, and the
#       fixed rtx.volumetrics.enableFogMaxDistanceRemap). It is a look key, so it is not part of
#       this A/B - but it is the one thing in the shipped profile whose value can move DURING a
#       session without the plugin touching it: the density follows the game's own fog end
#       (data/timecyc.dat FarClp), which SA cross-fades between weather types, and the runtime
#       re-derives sigma from the frame's fog state (rtx_global_volumetrics.cpp:470-540). A scripted
#       instant weather change therefore moves the medium in one frame, and plugin/src/smooth.cpp
#       cannot rate-limit it: rtxconf.cpp refuses every "volumetrics." key at runtime (kUnsafeKeys,
#       :245-252 - pushing that subsystem mid-run killed the bridge server once). If a haze pop on a
#       weather change shows up in this A/B, the fix is the game's data (timecyc rows), not a key
#       here. Density arithmetic: python3 tools/fog-budget.py config/rtx.conf.nopops.
#
# HOW TO READ THE A/B: scripts/lighting-test.sh, driving under the lamps at dusk with
# [clock] apply=1. Two things the plugin cannot fix from its own side: the shadow-direction jump
# when a distant-light handle is replaced, and the ambient step when rtx.skyBrightness moves. If
# a change now lags visibly (a lamp taking a quarter second to reach full brightness), lower
# maxNumFramesToAccumulate and maxAccumulationFrames first and newFrameWeight last: the last one
# is the whole-image blend and is also the one that can ghost when the camera moves.
#
# Read the runtime's own log after the first launch of any profile:
#   grep -A80 'Effective RtxOption values' <game>/rtx-remix/logs/remix-dxvk.log
# The graphics-preset layer (rtx.graphicsPreset = 1, not Custom) sits ABOVE rtx.conf for the keys
# it sets: if a key here does not read as changed in the Remix UI, the preset owns it.
#
rtx.enableVolumetricLighting = True
rtx.minReplacementTextureMipMapLevel = 1
rtx.restirGI.boilingFilterRemoveReservoirThreshold = 30
rtx.decalTextures = 0x0270E41E9BF89516, 0x11C6FD244CAED049, 0x1A1B9A4E6CAF5E9E, 0x1A2F5F13EC1D6CDD, 0x1B91964934D3E1B1, 0x20D2BD9A8F8CB7F1, 0x26626F3D0B9542FD, 0x2E80B8878F003D7A, 0x35BF4E34E77A0B7C, 0x38FA6E19237E82FE, 0x39E5406DC4D3006D, 0x3F3E25323C1E0220, 0x432E891037A5F4CE, 0x45ABC0B544E897FF, 0x470163CEBDD9080F, 0x4B0B6CE7E032DD29, 0x56190D6EE7379FE5, 0x5B1F839051230514, 0x5F3832E42403337A, 0x62DC87C416A654CD, 0x64DC83584D2F7779, 0x66EFA5FE769C8102, 0x6BD6FA2D62E32EDA, 0x73AC780C8EDE6485, 0x76CF04376B04B8B4, 0x78F9BBFB79156E88, 0x791CFC283E2D00E1, 0x86E7F5D9BDCB6871, 0x960E9DA1FBC8D55C, 0x98F1B88719A0D2C8, 0xA244C9C2D5956E1A, 0xA29AEC4A3D81DFDC, 0xA6D7045F8740A5DF, 0xB97DAA02F39D2524, 0xBBB8BF8C92DEC94F, 0xBE686B6B6B8AE7ED, 0xC1DEE32B1A6EDBE3, 0xC7593BF9215112DD, 0xC8EDDFB620CA8A5F, 0xCA4EAF478D3E998C, 0xD0A66A65C7528968, 0xDB4F6AFB517CAA97, 0xDD0B477219193EA2, 0xE099A3A212027ADE, 0xE857B38281B72432, 0xFC343E2104C444F9, 0xFDFC58D603A94554
rtx.restirGI.validateVisibilityChange = False
# n16 audit found this key DEAD in 1.6.1 (the option is rtx.volumetrics.enableFogMaxDistanceRemap,
# default True), so the line here had never done anything and max-distance remap had always run at
# its default. n17 fixed the name and writes the value the whole fog block depends on explicitly:
# the behaviour is unchanged (True is the default), but the fog chain is now readable in one place
# and the dead-key allow-list in config/rtx-dead-keys.txt lost this entry. See the n17 block at the
# end of this file.
rtx.volumetrics.enableFogMaxDistanceRemap = True
rtx.dlfg.enable = False
# n24 (cause 3, docs/FINDINGS.md "Night realism"): the fallback light is OFF, in every
# profile we ship. rtx_light_manager.cpp:249-330 creates it when the mode says so, and the
# runtime's own tooltip for the mode (config/rtx-options-1.6.1.tsv:299) says this outright:
#   "this option should be set to Never for 'production' builds of Remix creations to avoid
#    the fallback light from appearing in games unintentionally in cases where no lights
#    exist (which is the default behavior when set to NoLightsPresent)"
# The default is mode 1 = NoLightsPresent, and this file carried rtx.fallbackLightType = 1
# (Sphere) with rtx.fallbackLightRadius = 3, which puts a 3 m sphere AT THE CAMERA
# (mainCamera.getPosition() + fallbackLightPositionOffset). Measured with
# tools/night_ambient.py: at the runtime's default radiance 1.6,1.8,2.0 (luminance 1.786)
# and 6 m it delivers 0.4430 against the moon's 0.0515 - it out-lights the only real source
# in the night sky 8.6:1 - and the scene's away/under ratio goes 7.1 % -> 26.3 % with both
# ends clipping to white. It is off during gameplay (405+ external lights at the probe) and
# ON in menus and in the first seconds of a run: that is the "lit by nothing" look.
rtx.fallbackLightMode = 0
#   0 = Never. The radiance is zeroed too: mode 0 never creates the light, but the runtime's
#   default 1.6,1.8,2 stays armed for anyone who flips the mode in the Remix UI (which writes
#   user.conf, and user.conf sits ABOVE this file). 0,0,0 is the option table's own minimum.
rtx.fallbackLightRadiance = 0, 0, 0
# The shape it used to have, commented out so the 8.6:1 above can be re-measured:
#   rtx.fallbackLightRadius = 3
#   rtx.fallbackLightType = 1
rtx.ignoreTextures = 0x04931B9EC8F68722, 0x0748B4991EA9E481, 0x0801DDFEFD4AD85F, 0x12327A08E725DEE0, 0x188BFFED5020950B, 0x4DBD066761A29F5B, 0x63F56BAD1D42704D, 0x650AB0CEFBAFD10F, 0x6D2446E17BAC3050, 0x6F0AB36E2C7DB1A6, 0x72F44E5A77F84F90, 0x8CA564E68A98C4A7, 0x8F34BF39FA23F39D, 0x9D6A9F7B9355F09F, 0xB1B3C04B07B5ACEA, 0xB5C86BEE8E84357E, 0xBC9E2A1A5E437634, 0xBCCFDFAAE0F4E2AF, 0xE2D32425EB3234C0
rtx.enableFirstBounceLobeProbabilityDithering = False
rtx.restirGI.boilingFilterMinThreshold = 15
# rtx.fallbackLightType = 1   n24: commented out with the radius above - mode 0 never creates the light, and leaving the shape armed re-arms a camera-welded sphere the moment anyone touches the mode
rtx.allowFSE = True
rtx.usePostFilter = False
rtx.minOpaqueSpecularLobeSamplingProbability = 0.05
rtx.restirGI.sampleStealingJitter = 3
rtx.di.stealBoundaryPixelSamplesWhenOutsideOfScreen = False
rtx.postfx.enableVignette = False
rtx.opacityMicromap.showAdvancedOptions = True

# s32: carried from the live <game>/rtx.conf - it had these three, this shipped profile did
# not, so `tools/rtxprofile.sh current` used to drop them. The hair-card cutouts below are
# exactly what opacity micromaps are for, so every profile we ship should carry them.
rtx.opacityMicromap.enable = True
rtx.opacityMicromap.cache.minFreeVidmemMBToNotAllocate = 1536
rtx.opacityMicromap.cache.maxBudgetSizeMB = 1024
rtx.restirGI.virtualSampleMaxDistanceRatio = 0.5
rtx.uiTextures = 0x02FB7ADBE915018A, 0x1D3D385E969C83FA, 0x3259CE3885F90642, 0x3781F525BBBD9FBD, 0x3FC78C823061BDB0, 0x5825FA46CF812787, 0x5970DA3808FA8128, 0x9D36811F7D6CB321, 0xC412E7D486FF9E5C, 0xEDAA7402F2B0E4BB
rtx.restirGI.historyDiscardStrength = 2
rtx.pathMaxBounces = 2
# n24 (V4, docs/STATUS.md): back to the runtime's own default. The option table ships this
# False (config/rtx-options-1.6.1.tsv:280); the True here came from 88e8cd1 with no
# measurement behind it, and the tooltip is the mechanism of the complaint: "Translucent
# objects hit by indirect rays will not alter ray direction, just change the ray throughput"
# - indirect rays keep their direction THROUGH the sea, so the sky and the lamp pools arrive
# under the pier and the sea glows with nothing under it.
rtx.enableTransmissionApproximationInIndirectRays = False
rtx.freeCameraSpeed = 50
rtx.stochasticAlphaBlendOpacityThreshold = 0.2
rtx.lightmapTextures = 0xB6E06CE024FE9111
# rtx.localtonemap.shadows moved into the n16 block at the end of this file (2.5 -> 1.0),
# so that the tonemap keys this task ships sit together with their reasons.
rtx.dlfg.enablePresentMetering = False
rtx.instanceOverrideInstanceIdx = 2147483647
rtx.useNewGuiInputMethod = False
rtx.autoExposure.evMinValue = 0
rtx.restirGI.useVirtualSample = False
rtx.restirGI.useAdaptiveTemporalHistory = True
rtx.emissiveIntensity = 1.0
rtx.di.enableDenoiserConfidence = False
rtx.skyBoxTextures = 0x294AFA03D0A0EC8F, 0x56F9D8C7499BC624, 0x79452E6A6F0D6C0E, 0x7A4F053D4D0C4437, 0x9B80DC0B3042D274, 0xA3DB5B6EB9628265, 0xBBC7C2A811DC27AF, 0xD4B43B810BECAFE8, 0xD90E0FB3CC373950, 0xDB44893153AB9DFB, 0xEB05F3829A0BA3B0, 0xEBABE67E3BB17A9D
rtx.di.permutationSamplingNthFrame = 0
# n04 (2026-11-12b finding A): the runtime default is 0 = off, and 1 was ours. It
# re-randomises which lights a pixel draws every frame; over the ~400-light pool
# this plugin keeps live that is the closest thing in this renderer to "lights
# flicker in and out of existence when the camera rotates". See
# .strata/notes-n04-lights-always-on.md step 2B and docs/FINDINGS.md.
rtx.ignoreGameDirectionalLights = True
rtx.restirGI.useSampleStealing = 1
rtx.particleTextures = 0x05D81D806AE2DE92, 0x166C27331EA67091, 0x2D558BC31F67FD1D, 0x2F580E4763FAB662, 0x3F66C5D5F91C3708, 0x448152B6FE9F348C, 0x44AF0EF987753198, 0x4EEAC3D05034C372, 0x76BD5D19A139B6BC, 0x7A8BAF2D1E21CC24, 0x9A8E8191471533D5, 0xB51546930E6B5738, 0xB573F6EEBBA1EC5D, 0xBE5F9FBE5DF344A8, 0xC70DC799C5BFFE65, 0xC97B2D2D38F5CF97, 0xCE919FA9A67900C4, 0xD4220861A515C770, 0xEA35A9BFBDAE3C3B, 0xEF687DB43CD24DE3

# n12 (test-2026-11-12c V6): "exhaust smoke renders as bright pink/white glowing puffs".
# Three of the game's smoke sprites were in no category at all - effectsPC.txd's smoke5
# (0xC70DC799C5BFFE65) and smoke4 (0x3F66C5D5F91C3708), the two textures of the fx systems
# the game itself calls smoke50lit / smoke30lit, and effectsPC.txd's bullethitsmoke1
# (0x2D558BC31F67FD1D). Everything else the particle manager draws (collisionsmoke, smoke /
# smokeII_3, bullethitsmoke, fireball6, the wake and splash sprites) was already listed, so
# these three were the odd ones out: rendered as ordinary alpha-blended geometry rather
# than as particles. Hashes are xxh3 over mip-0 of the texture, reproducible with
#   python3 tools/remix_texhash.py models/effectsPC.txd --conf config/rtx.conf
# Coronas (coronamoon, coronastar, coronareflect) were considered for this list and left
# out: the Particle category does not touch the emissive term at all (it moves the instance
# to the unordered TLAS and re-orients billboards in indirect rays), so adding the sprites
# behind every 2dFx glow in the game would be a wide change with no measured benefit here.
rtx.emissiveBlendOverrideEmissiveIntensity = 0.06

# n12 (V6, and half of V5): the mechanism behind both complaints is one code path. Any draw
# call whose D3D blend mode is semi/additive is translated to an emissive surface
# (rtx_options.h:935 enableEmissiveBlendModeTranslation, default True) and its material's
# emissive is then FORCED to this value (rtx_instance_manager.cpp:1132-1138, gated by
# enableEmissiveBlendEmissiveOverride, default True) with the albedo as the emissive colour.
# The runtime default is 0.2 and its own tooltip says what it is for: "Adjust this if
# particles for example look overly bright globally." Measured on the 2026-11-12 frames
# (tools/frame_light_measure.py, build/tmp/video-2026-11-12): the puffs average RGB
# [172,57,80] - pink, because the emissive term is the sprite's own colour with no scene
# light in it - and their high-pass luminance is 31-96 above the ground they rise from, so
# they are brighter than the road under a street lamp. A puff of soot scatters; it does not
# emit. 0.06 is a 3.3x cut: the sprite keeps enough self-lit look to read as a glowing
# plume (the game's additive blend is still translated) while the scene's lights, including
# the new vehicle lamps, become the term that decides how bright it is. It is one dial over
# every emissive-blended sprite in the game, which is also why the 15-unit (30 m) red tail
# corona of V5 gets dimmer with it; the visible bulb stays, only its glow shrinks.
# Set it back to 0.2 to A/B the effect.

# s32: the hillside vegetation (grass/bush/fern/leaf cards) comes out as soft scribbled lines.
# These are the alpha-cutout card textures of the game's procedural-vegetation objects: every
# texture in the dictionaries those objects reference, kept only where the texture really has
# cutout content (>=5 % fully transparent or >=5 % mid-alpha texels; the opaque bark/dirt in the
# same dictionaries is left alone). Regenerate with:
#   python3 tools/vegetation_hashes.py models/gta3.img --emit rtx.hairCardTextures
# (the dictionary allow-list is the tool's default; --verbose prints the per-texture alpha table).
# Measured on the mip-0 alpha: gta_proc_rushes gras03Si 76.5 % alpha 0 / 20.8 % mid / 2.7 %
# opaque; gta_proc_grassland oak2b 89.8 / 10.2 / 0; gta_proc_bush newtreeleaves128 30.2 / 20.6 /
# 49.2. The cards are painted with soft gradients and not one of them is in any rtx.conf category
# list today, so the runtime blends them instead of alpha-testing them - which is what the
# hair-card tooltip is for ("Textures in this category will preserve fine texture detail, render
# as cutouts instead of alpha blends, and disable backface culling"), and it is also what makes
# the opacity micromaps apply to them ("a ground densely covered with grass blades ... multiple
# trees with a lot of foliage").
# If the vegetation looks unchanged in game, this line may be too long for the parser: retry with
# the procobj-only subset (45 hashes, 922 chars) - .strata/notes-s32-water-vegetation.md.
